Conventional fabric inspection is typically performed after knitting, meaning that persistent needle or yarn abnormalities often accumulate substantial material waste before discovery. To address this issue, we present a defect detection system that integrates real-time inspection directly into the circular knitting process. The system combines rotation-activated detection, image acquisition, edge inference, and local machine stop control to limit defective fabric production. To enable efficient inference on resource-constrained edge devices, we propose \model, a lightweight detector guided by systematic observations of knitting defect morphologies. It incorporates a morphology-aware context encoding (MCE) module to capture the spatial continuity of elongated horizontal and vertical defects, and a gated interpolation upsampling (GIU) module for content-adaptive feature reconstruction across diverse scales. On a self-collected dataset, \model achieves 97.2\% mAP@50 and 65.5\% mAP@50:95, outperforming YOLOv8n by 6.7 and 7.1 percentage points, respectively. After INT8 quantization, the model maintains 96.7\% mAP@50 with an inference throughput of 23 FPS on the edge device. These results demonstrate the potential of the proposed system for scalable real-time defect detection on circular knitting machines in the textile industry.
